17:
In the statement "With the cessation of neither-painful-nor-pleasant feeling, unworldly happiness arises; with the cessation of unworldly happiness, neither-painful-nor-pleasant feeling arises," it does not mean that one enters and dwells in the third jhāna because of the cessation of the fourth jhāna. The meaning here should be understood fully as: emerging from the fourth jhāna, one enters the third jhāna; emerging from the third jhāna, one enters the fourth jhāna. "Unhappiness (domanassa) arises" refers to the unhappiness caused by an inferior jhāna (or by falling away from a jhāna). It refers to the malleability (kammanīyabhāva) of the mind that has emerged from attainment.

"Rapture born of seclusion" refers to the rapture present in those two jhānas (the first and second). "The place where the shadow is abandoned" means the area where the shadow is left behind. What does this mean? It means that where there is shadow, there is no sunlight; where there is sunlight, there is no shadow.

"Unworldly happiness" (nirāmisa sukha) is the happiness of the third jhāna. "Neither-painful-nor-pleasant" (adukkhamasukha) is the feeling of the fourth jhāna.

"I am one without clinging" means "I am one who has suppressed (the defilements)." "Suitable for Nibbāna" refers to the practice that is beneficial to Nibbāna. Does not the vision of the path (maggadassana) arise by drying up craving for all things? Why then is this practice called beneficial to Nibbāna? It is called a beneficial practice by virtue of not clinging to and not grasping all things. "Accuses" means to disparage with conceit. "Pubbantānudiṭṭhi" refers to the eighteen types of wrong views concerning the past. "Aparantānudiṭṭhi" refers to the forty-four types of wrong views concerning the future. "It is called clinging" means that because the grasping of "I am" is included within the view of self-identity (sakkāyadiṭṭhi), it is referred to as clinging to views (diṭṭhupādāna).

"The peaceful, supreme state" refers to Nibbāna, the supreme state which is peaceful because defilements are stilled. Regarding the "six bases of contact," the Blessed One showed Nibbāna by the rejection of two bases in this passage: "Where the eye ceases and the perception of form ceases, that base should be known."

"Where water, earth, fire, and air find no footing;
From there the currents (of craving) turn back;
Here the round (of rebirth) does not revolve;
Here name and form cease without remainder."

In this verse, Nibbāna is shown by the rejection of formations (saṅkhāra).
